1
00:01:15,576 --> 00:01:19,411
Turn one more time to get it.

2
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:24,111
Got it now, I think.

3
00:01:36,697 --> 00:01:38,165
Huh. Well...

4
00:01:41,735 --> 00:01:44,432
What? What?

5
00:01:44,605 --> 00:01:46,631
Oh.

6
00:01:46,807 --> 00:01:49,072
Oh, hello, Duncan.

7
00:01:49,242 --> 00:01:54,840
Oh, no, the disposal
just tried to eat my broom.

8
00:01:55,315 --> 00:01:57,216
Yeah, yeah.

9
00:01:57,384 --> 00:01:58,909
What?

10
00:01:59,319 --> 00:02:03,620
Have you got a blind date
over there or something for me?

11
00:02:05,692 --> 00:02:10,995
No, I liked... What
was her name?

12
00:02:11,365 --> 00:02:14,563
Alice? Yeah. Yeah.
Clara's Aunt Alice.

13
00:02:14,735 --> 00:02:19,173
Yeah, I liked her fine. I
liked 14 of her 15 cats.

14
00:02:20,407 --> 00:02:24,742
But there was fur all over
everything, including Alice.

15
00:02:24,911 --> 00:02:28,040
She kissed me good night
and I looked like the Wolf Man.

16
00:02:30,484 --> 00:02:35,218
Well, all right. I'll be
there at 6:00. Bye.

17
00:02:43,397 --> 00:02:44,922
Good afternoon, sir.
How are you today?

18
00:02:45,098 --> 00:02:46,293
My name is Carl M. Fuqua.

19
00:02:46,466 --> 00:02:48,799
I'm here on behalf of the
Willow Springs Boys Club.

20
00:02:49,002 --> 00:02:52,461
Oh, wonderful cause.
I gave at the office.

21
00:02:53,407 --> 00:02:54,431
Isn't this your office?

22
00:02:54,608 --> 00:02:57,271
Oh, it's one of them. I
have a chain like Kmart.

23
00:02:58,412 --> 00:03:02,008
- Ah. Ha, ha.
- I'm late for an appointment.

24
00:03:08,989 --> 00:03:11,720
Are you all right? - I think so.

25
00:03:11,892 --> 00:03:13,884
- Here. I'll call a doctor.
- Ooh!

26
00:03:14,928 --> 00:03:16,590
No, no. No. I'm fine, really.

27
00:03:16,763 --> 00:03:18,391
- Are you sure?
- I think so.

28
00:03:18,799 --> 00:03:21,200
Nothing seems to be
broken. Heh-heh. Thanks.

29
00:03:22,369 --> 00:03:23,894
Yeah.

30
00:04:04,745 --> 00:04:08,512
- Benjamin, it is so good to see you.
- Don't you look well?

31
00:04:08,682 --> 00:04:11,675
- Well, thank you.
- Ha, ha.

32
00:04:11,852 --> 00:04:13,184
Why am I here?

33
00:04:13,787 --> 00:04:15,619
Still expecting your
questions to be answered

34
00:04:15,789 --> 00:04:17,690
the minute you ask
them. Aren't you, Ben?

35
00:04:18,191 --> 00:04:19,682
Set in my ways.

36
00:04:19,860 --> 00:04:21,123
Before we get down to business,

37
00:04:21,294 --> 00:04:23,263
I have a young,
impetuous Montrachet

38
00:04:23,430 --> 00:04:25,126
I have been waiting
for a chance to crack.

39
00:04:25,298 --> 00:04:28,791
- Why, I'd just as soon...
- Oh, I insist.

40
00:04:31,905 --> 00:04:36,900
Benjamin, this isn't just
wine, this is nectar of the gods.

41
00:04:38,445 --> 00:04:41,882
Oh. You collect the glasses
and Duncan collects the wine.

42
00:04:42,048 --> 00:04:45,075
- Aren't you the perfect couple?
- Ha, ha.

43
00:04:45,252 --> 00:04:49,553
And like all perfect couples, we
have come to the same conclusion

44
00:04:49,723 --> 00:04:52,056
at precisely the same moment.

45
00:04:52,225 --> 00:04:54,265
- We want a divorce.
- And we'd like you to help us.

46
00:04:56,830 --> 00:04:59,527
- How's that?
- Well, you see, Ben,

47
00:04:59,699 --> 00:05:03,329
Duncan and I care about each
other deeply, and we always will.

48
00:05:03,503 --> 00:05:06,735
But our marriage,
well, it's like a tree.

49
00:05:06,907 --> 00:05:08,136
Though our roots go deep,

50
00:05:08,308 --> 00:05:10,573
the branches have been
diverging for quite some time.

51
00:05:11,645 --> 00:05:13,307
A tree?

52
00:05:14,014 --> 00:05:16,040
What are you...?
What are you talk...?

53
00:05:16,216 --> 00:05:19,414
No, never mind. Just
settle your differences.

54
00:05:19,586 --> 00:05:24,024
Ha, ha. We're psychiatrists,
Ben. We're relationship experts.

55
00:05:24,191 --> 00:05:28,754
Ours is in a state of
transition from perfect to over.

56
00:05:30,197 --> 00:05:32,928
Well, I'm not a divorce lawyer.

57
00:05:33,233 --> 00:05:36,670
Oh, but we want this to be amiable.
There are no children to consider.

58
00:05:36,837 --> 00:05:38,517
Yeah. We just need
help with the paperwork.

59
00:05:38,672 --> 00:05:39,912
Well, you have to understand...

60
00:05:40,073 --> 00:05:43,771
The thing is, the last
thing we want, um,

61
00:05:43,944 --> 00:05:48,405
are legal sharks bloodying
our tranquil divorce waters

62
00:05:48,582 --> 00:05:51,177
with greed and vindictiveness.

63
00:05:51,351 --> 00:05:52,910
Please help us, Ben.

64
00:05:53,420 --> 00:05:54,911
As friends?

65
00:06:01,661 --> 00:06:03,425
Why are they getting a divorce?

66
00:06:03,630 --> 00:06:08,159
Well, the way they put it to
me, their marriage is like a tree.

67
00:06:08,335 --> 00:06:12,329
The roots go deep but
the limbs are either rotten

68
00:06:12,505 --> 00:06:15,202
or growing every which
way. I can't remember.

69
00:06:15,375 --> 00:06:17,015
Sounds like they
use their own fertilizer.

70
00:06:18,378 --> 00:06:19,869
Yeah. I'll get it.

71
00:06:26,253 --> 00:06:27,277
Hi.

72
00:06:27,454 --> 00:06:29,047
- Benjamin Matlock?
- Front and center.

73
00:06:29,222 --> 00:06:31,062
I have a registered
letter for you. Sign there.

74
00:06:31,224 --> 00:06:33,216
Oh, okay.

75
00:06:35,629 --> 00:06:36,961
Yeah.

76
00:06:37,731 --> 00:06:38,960
Have a good one.

77
00:06:39,266 --> 00:06:40,632
You have a better one.

78
00:06:48,508 --> 00:06:49,942
Who's that from?

79
00:06:50,110 --> 00:06:54,946
It's from some
lawyer here in Atlanta.

80
00:06:58,785 --> 00:07:00,083
You've got to be kidding.

81
00:07:00,253 --> 00:07:03,655
"This is to inform you that
my client, Carl M. Fuqua,

82
00:07:03,823 --> 00:07:05,086
is bringing suit against you

83
00:07:05,258 --> 00:07:07,386
for the dangerous
condition of your property."

84
00:07:07,560 --> 00:07:08,789
Dangerous?

85
00:07:08,962 --> 00:07:12,091
- Do you see anything dangerous?
- "Which resulted in serious injury

86
00:07:12,265 --> 00:07:14,757
- to my client."
- Ah, there was no injury.

87
00:07:14,935 --> 00:07:17,700
"We suggest you turn this matter
over to your insurance company."

88
00:07:17,871 --> 00:07:20,397
You wanna tell me
what this is all about?

89
00:07:20,573 --> 00:07:25,876
This guy, Fuqua, comes here,
collecting for some boys club.

90
00:07:26,046 --> 00:07:29,039
- And you showed him the door.
- Well, yeah.

91
00:07:29,215 --> 00:07:30,495
Yeah. And the next thing I know,

92
00:07:30,650 --> 00:07:33,415
he's in a heap at the
bottom of the stairs.

93
00:07:33,586 --> 00:07:35,953
- Did he seem hurt?
- No.

94
00:07:36,122 --> 00:07:37,962
No. He was a little
woozy when he first got up.

95
00:07:38,124 --> 00:07:40,650
I asked him if he wanted to
see a doctor. He said he was fine.

96
00:07:40,827 --> 00:07:44,161
Then he walked away
under his own power.

97
00:07:44,931 --> 00:07:46,593
That's just insurance
fraud, that's all.

98
00:07:46,766 --> 00:07:48,735
Sounds like it to me. Ah, wait.

99
00:07:48,902 --> 00:07:50,662
Insurance fraud or not,
you've gotta respond.

100
00:07:50,804 --> 00:07:52,170
Let me handle this for you.

101
00:07:52,339 --> 00:07:55,207
Once I finish with him,
he won't know what hit him.

102
00:07:55,675 --> 00:07:57,405
Ben, you folks got a problem?

103
00:07:57,978 --> 00:08:00,004
Nothing we can't handle, Billy.

104
00:08:00,180 --> 00:08:01,808
I figured something
must be going on,

105
00:08:01,982 --> 00:08:05,009
otherwise you'd have given my boy,
Cliff, a job by now, like you promised.

106
00:08:05,185 --> 00:08:06,949
I said I'd try, Billy.

107
00:08:07,120 --> 00:08:09,749
Leanne and I just started
up a new practice here

108
00:08:09,923 --> 00:08:11,858
and there's not enough
work for three people.

109
00:08:12,025 --> 00:08:14,051
You better not be backing
out of our deal, Ben.

110
00:08:14,227 --> 00:08:16,321
- You owe me.
- I don't owe you, Billy.

111
00:08:16,496 --> 00:08:18,362
He got your son out
from under a murder wrap.

112
00:08:18,531 --> 00:08:21,660
- Isn't that worth something?
- You just don't understand, Leanne.

113
00:08:21,835 --> 00:08:25,533
Ben got out, got away from
home, went to law school.

114
00:08:25,705 --> 00:08:27,105
He's a rich lawyer now.

115
00:08:28,008 --> 00:08:30,807
I had to stay home. I'm
just a poor working man.

116
00:08:30,977 --> 00:08:33,606
My sister, Lucy, loved
him in high school.

117
00:08:33,780 --> 00:08:37,717
He cast her aside like an old
sock. She never got over of it.

118
00:08:37,884 --> 00:08:39,819
- Dad?
- I never cast her aside.

119
00:08:39,986 --> 00:08:42,683
- I just started dating old man...
- Old man McKnight's girl.

120
00:08:42,856 --> 00:08:45,621
Said you liked the
dimple on her chin.

121
00:08:45,792 --> 00:08:48,159
Lucy cried and cried.

122
00:08:48,328 --> 00:08:50,923
I wound up having to take
care of Lucy all these years,

123
00:08:51,097 --> 00:08:52,929
and you got to law school.

124
00:08:53,099 --> 00:08:54,328
I work, Leanne.

125
00:08:54,701 --> 00:08:57,500
I scraped and I sent my
boy, Cliff, to law school.

126
00:08:57,670 --> 00:08:58,899
And he owes me.

127
00:08:59,072 --> 00:09:01,735
I don't owe you, Billy. You
can't move here to Willow Springs

128
00:09:01,908 --> 00:09:03,570
and bring that boy
of yours with you

129
00:09:03,743 --> 00:09:06,303
and hold your sister
over my head like this.

130
00:09:06,479 --> 00:09:09,540
The least you can do is
give my boy a job, Ben.

131
00:09:13,219 --> 00:09:14,744
Ben?

132
00:09:15,288 --> 00:09:16,620
Ben?

133
00:09:16,790 --> 00:09:18,656
Well, what about
the place in Aspen?

134
00:09:18,825 --> 00:09:22,023
Oh, Duncan should have
it. He's the one who skis.

135
00:09:22,495 --> 00:09:25,192
Not since I broke
my leg two years ago.

136
00:09:25,432 --> 00:09:26,456
You take it, darling.

137
00:09:26,633 --> 00:09:28,659
Aspen's always been your
place of psychic renewal.

138
00:09:30,270 --> 00:09:31,294
You're right.

139
00:09:31,471 --> 00:09:33,940
But you must take the
time share on Hilton Head.

140
00:09:35,642 --> 00:09:37,202
Look forward to
working on my golf game.

141
00:09:37,343 --> 00:09:39,972
You know, I found my father's
old golf clubs in the garage.

142
00:09:40,146 --> 00:09:41,944
Look at this, Ben.

143
00:09:43,149 --> 00:09:44,378
Solid persimmon.

144
00:09:44,818 --> 00:09:46,514
Oh, that's beautiful.

145
00:09:46,686 --> 00:09:47,984
Yeah. Yeah.

146
00:09:48,154 --> 00:09:51,090
Well, I wish you
could work things out,

147
00:09:51,257 --> 00:09:53,920
but, uh, if you'll give me
your financial statements,

148
00:09:54,094 --> 00:09:56,359
I can wrap this up
in a couple days.

149
00:09:56,529 --> 00:09:58,930
I'll have everything ready
for you tomorrow morning.

150
00:09:59,099 --> 00:10:00,431
Thanks again for helping, Ben.

151
00:10:00,600 --> 00:10:05,334
Oh, well, if everybody's like you,
divorce lawyers would starve to death.

152
00:10:05,505 --> 00:10:07,599
- Yeah, I'll help.
- Ha, ha.

153
00:10:12,412 --> 00:10:14,347
I love you.

154
00:10:15,048 --> 00:10:16,641
I know.

155
00:10:49,149 --> 00:10:50,947
Duncan? Your breakfast is ready.

156
00:10:51,117 --> 00:10:52,483
Be right there, darling.

157
00:10:58,124 --> 00:11:02,391
- You are really something.
- So are you, darling.

158
00:11:04,297 --> 00:11:08,701
Now, sit down before
your coffee gets cold.

159
00:11:12,472 --> 00:11:15,408
Ah, you know, this is fascinating.
Before we decided to get divorced,

160
00:11:15,575 --> 00:11:17,942
I doubt you would have
done anything like this for me.

161
00:11:18,111 --> 00:11:20,239
And if you had, I probably
wouldn't have noticed.

162
00:11:20,413 --> 00:11:21,779
What's that, sweetheart?

163
00:11:21,948 --> 00:11:23,828
I'm saying that now that
we're getting divorced,

164
00:11:23,983 --> 00:11:26,043
we're finally able to
appreciate each other.

165
00:11:26,252 --> 00:11:30,280
I mean, not as husband
and wife but as individuals.

166
00:11:30,456 --> 00:11:33,984
The unique and special
people we really are.

167
00:11:34,694 --> 00:11:37,562
I know my eyes have been opened.

168
00:11:43,436 --> 00:11:47,134
I'm real sorry about my
dad. I know he can be a pain.

169
00:11:47,307 --> 00:11:48,434
Yeah.

170
00:11:48,608 --> 00:11:53,706
Well, simple uncontested divorce
like this is good to cut your teeth on.

171
00:11:53,880 --> 00:11:55,075
- Not much money.
- No, no.

172
00:11:55,248 --> 00:11:56,944
But good experience.

173
00:11:57,116 --> 00:11:59,745
That's what I'm here for.
Now, who are the Farmingtons?

174
00:11:59,919 --> 00:12:01,820
- Oh, they're the nicest people.
- Yeah.

175
00:12:01,988 --> 00:12:04,685
They've done real well for
themselves. Psychiatrists.

176
00:12:04,857 --> 00:12:07,827
- Oh.
- Yeah. Friendly, nice.

177
00:12:12,699 --> 00:12:14,099
Clara, my God.

178
00:12:14,267 --> 00:12:17,999
- Snakes for a snake, you worm.
- Clara.

179
00:12:18,171 --> 00:12:21,369
You haven't been to Aspen
in over two years, my foot.

180
00:12:21,541 --> 00:12:24,340
It's not like you to
overreact. Clara.

181
00:12:24,510 --> 00:12:27,537
Overreact? If anything,
I am underreacting,

182
00:12:27,714 --> 00:12:29,239
considering the fact
that my husband,

183
00:12:29,415 --> 00:12:31,895
who was supposed to be at a
professional conference in Chicago,

184
00:12:32,051 --> 00:12:36,250
actually spent last February
1st through 10th in Aspen,

185
00:12:36,422 --> 00:12:40,086
charging champagne
dinners and massages,

186
00:12:40,260 --> 00:12:43,753
and ladies ski clothes for some
bimbo he was romancing in my house.

187
00:12:43,930 --> 00:12:45,193
It's not a bimbo.

188
00:12:45,898 --> 00:12:50,427
It ended a long time ago and it
has nothing to do with our divorce.

189
00:12:51,337 --> 00:12:54,068
That's what you think.

190
00:12:56,309 --> 00:12:59,040
Okay, now what are we doing?

191
00:12:59,779 --> 00:13:01,941
- Excuse me.
- Clara!

192
00:13:02,115 --> 00:13:03,606
What are you doing?

193
00:13:03,783 --> 00:13:06,753
Renegotiating our
property settlement.

194
00:13:07,553 --> 00:13:09,488
Clara.

195
00:13:09,656 --> 00:13:13,650
- Oh, Clara, darling.
- Don't you "darling" me, you snake.

196
00:13:14,861 --> 00:13:16,454
See?

197
00:13:17,030 --> 00:13:18,760
- That's overreacting.
- Oh.

198
00:13:18,931 --> 00:13:21,298
You aren't even giving
me a chance to explain.

199
00:13:21,467 --> 00:13:24,596
And Clara, this is not
very adult behavior.

200
00:13:24,771 --> 00:13:29,232
Ah-ah. That side's
yours, this is mine.

201
00:13:33,146 --> 00:13:35,615
Don't cross that line.

202
00:13:35,948 --> 00:13:39,976
- Give me that.
- Okay. Catch.

203
00:13:46,592 --> 00:13:50,188
Regressing, are we? Okay.

204
00:13:50,363 --> 00:13:52,696
You want infantile?

205
00:13:52,865 --> 00:13:54,333
- Duncan.
- You got it.

206
00:13:54,500 --> 00:13:55,798
Duncan, don't!

207
00:14:05,211 --> 00:14:08,238
- This is a nice house.
- Oh, yeah.

208
00:14:08,414 --> 00:14:10,280
Wow.

209
00:14:15,321 --> 00:14:17,256
Do you hear something?

210
00:14:17,423 --> 00:14:19,358
Ah, probably the television.

211
00:14:20,460 --> 00:14:22,019
What the hell do you want?

212
00:14:28,801 --> 00:14:31,600
No, no. DUNCAN:
That woman is deranged.

213
00:14:31,771 --> 00:14:33,967
Grab her! Grab her, Duncan.

214
00:14:34,140 --> 00:14:35,517
- I've got her.
- Let go of me.

215
00:14:35,541 --> 00:14:37,181
- Stop it.
- She's out of her mind.

216
00:14:37,276 --> 00:14:38,938
- You can't be acting this way.
- Ow!

217
00:14:39,112 --> 00:14:41,638
Duncan. Duncan.
Stop... No. Stop that!

218
00:14:41,814 --> 00:14:42,975
Well, she started it.

219
00:14:43,182 --> 00:14:45,515
I did not. He did.
Philandering creep.

220
00:14:45,685 --> 00:14:48,553
- Not freaking true.
- Wait a minute!

221
00:14:50,423 --> 00:14:53,791
Yesterday, you were acting
like you were planning a wedding,

222
00:14:53,960 --> 00:14:55,553
not a divorce.

223
00:14:55,728 --> 00:14:57,788
Now you're ready to kill one
another. What happened?

224
00:14:57,964 --> 00:15:00,126
- He had an affair.
- She's overreacting.

225
00:15:00,299 --> 00:15:02,359
- I'm going home.
- Wait, you can't go.

226
00:15:02,535 --> 00:15:03,867
You're our lawyer.

227
00:15:04,036 --> 00:15:06,938
Was your lawyer. I agreed
to sign on to do paperwork,

228
00:15:07,106 --> 00:15:09,632
not referee some
demolition derby.

229
00:15:09,809 --> 00:15:12,176
Get yourself another
lawyer. Get two.

230
00:15:12,345 --> 00:15:13,904
Come on, Cliff.

231
00:15:19,619 --> 00:15:22,111
You want infantile? You got it.

232
00:15:22,288 --> 00:15:23,984
Duncan, don't.

233
00:15:24,157 --> 00:15:26,956
- Guess this means I'm out of a job.
- We both are.

234
00:15:31,364 --> 00:15:34,823
Mr. Bostwick. My name
is Leanne Mclntyre.

235
00:15:35,001 --> 00:15:37,436
Dixieland Insurance
authorized me to represent them

236
00:15:37,603 --> 00:15:41,563
in your client's personal-injury
suit against Mr. Benjamin Matlock.

237
00:15:41,941 --> 00:15:46,470
The same Benjamin Matlock
who just happens to be your father.

238
00:15:48,314 --> 00:15:50,078
I, um...

239
00:15:50,416 --> 00:15:51,816
I checked you out.

240
00:15:51,984 --> 00:15:54,886
Good. Then I can lay
my cards on the table.

241
00:15:55,054 --> 00:15:56,613
Your client has no case.

242
00:15:56,789 --> 00:15:58,815
Now, we could go to
court, trade witnesses,

243
00:15:58,991 --> 00:16:01,825
waste a lot of time and money,
or we could settle right now.

244
00:16:02,595 --> 00:16:04,120
I'm listening.

245
00:16:04,297 --> 00:16:07,324
Granting that your client
sustained any injuries at all,

246
00:16:07,500 --> 00:16:12,097
which, from Mr. Matlock's description
of the accident, seems highly unlikely,

247
00:16:12,271 --> 00:16:16,834
we feel that the sum of $25,000 is
more than adequate compensation.

248
00:16:25,218 --> 00:16:27,084
Well, Mr. Bostwick?

249
00:16:29,622 --> 00:16:32,558
This must be a joke. I'm
just waiting on the punch line.

250
00:16:32,725 --> 00:16:34,819
I didn't come here to
play games, Mr. Bostwick.

251
00:16:35,828 --> 00:16:39,162
You're no fun at
all, are you? Okay.

252
00:16:39,565 --> 00:16:41,056
Let's get serious.

253
00:16:43,102 --> 00:16:45,571
Do you have a medical
degree, Leanne?

254
00:16:46,372 --> 00:16:48,068
May I call you Leanne?

255
00:16:48,508 --> 00:16:50,568
No, Mr. Bostwick, you may not.

256
00:16:51,077 --> 00:16:53,273
Cute. Real cute.

257
00:16:53,446 --> 00:16:54,470
All right.

258
00:16:54,647 --> 00:16:58,448
The insurance adjuster's already
found loose boards on your father's porch.

259
00:16:59,252 --> 00:17:02,245
The doctors I talked to tell
me that Carl Fuqua sustained

260
00:17:02,421 --> 00:17:05,914
serious, permanent
damage to his neck and spine.

261
00:17:07,727 --> 00:17:12,028
Your 25 grand wouldn't even take
care of the treatment he's had so far,

262
00:17:12,198 --> 00:17:14,963
much less what he's gonna
need for the rest of his life.

263
00:17:16,602 --> 00:17:19,333
I assume you have
a figure in mind.

264
00:17:19,505 --> 00:17:21,030
Let's hear it.

265
00:17:21,207 --> 00:17:24,575
A million dollars?
Is he kidding?

266
00:17:24,877 --> 00:17:27,197
If we don't come up with
something in the high six figures,

267
00:17:27,346 --> 00:17:29,076
he'll see us in court.

268
00:17:29,248 --> 00:17:31,240
I can't wait.

269
00:17:31,417 --> 00:17:33,716
A million dollars. Shyster.

270
00:17:33,886 --> 00:17:35,184
You still wanna take this case?

271
00:17:35,354 --> 00:17:37,152
- You betcha.
- Go get him.

272
00:17:37,323 --> 00:17:38,518
Excuse us.

273
00:17:39,025 --> 00:17:42,018
- Ben, we are chagrined.
- Mortified.

274
00:17:42,428 --> 00:17:44,021
Truly ashamed.

275
00:17:44,196 --> 00:17:46,665
Can we please come in?

276
00:17:47,733 --> 00:17:52,535
And all our repressed pain, anger,
and resentment came flooding forth

277
00:17:52,705 --> 00:17:56,642
- in one massive catharsis.
- Very liberating, actually.

278
00:17:56,809 --> 00:18:00,246
And now that we've worked
through it, we can move on.

279
00:18:00,413 --> 00:18:02,348
And we really
want you to help us.

280
00:18:03,015 --> 00:18:05,575
Oh, no, no, no.

281
00:18:05,751 --> 00:18:08,915
Each of you needs
a separate lawyer.

282
00:18:10,456 --> 00:18:12,482
What a good idea.

283
00:18:12,658 --> 00:18:14,786
Miss Mclntyre can represent me.

284
00:18:16,062 --> 00:18:18,628
Oh, well, I... Ben
can represent me.

285
00:18:18,798 --> 00:18:21,859
Clara, my darling, that's brilliant.
That would keep it all in the family.

286
00:18:22,034 --> 00:18:24,299
Oh, the last thing we want
are strangers coming in

287
00:18:24,470 --> 00:18:26,769
and making problems
where none exist.

288
00:18:26,939 --> 00:18:28,805
Sharks bloodying the water.

289
00:18:28,975 --> 00:18:31,911
No. We acknowledge
those negative feelings, Ben.

290
00:18:32,078 --> 00:18:34,013
But believe me, this
time, we really mean it.

291
00:18:34,180 --> 00:18:36,376
Oh, with you on board,
this divorce can be

292
00:18:36,549 --> 00:18:40,145
the positive, life-affirming
process we know it can be.

293
00:18:40,319 --> 00:18:43,016
I'm not sure this is the
right way to go about this.

294
00:18:43,656 --> 00:18:46,455
Oh, but you've already
done most of the work.

295
00:18:46,626 --> 00:18:48,424
Are you sure you
won't see it through?

296
00:18:52,898 --> 00:18:54,799
What do you think, Dad?

297
00:18:56,202 --> 00:18:58,535
I don't know. What do you think?

298
00:18:59,805 --> 00:19:01,034
So you fired my boy.

299
00:19:01,407 --> 00:19:04,673
I didn't fire him. The case
changed. He's not right for it.

300
00:19:04,844 --> 00:19:06,644
Said you'd give him a
job, then you fired him.

301
00:19:06,779 --> 00:19:09,578
I didn't fire him. He's
not right for the case.

302
00:19:09,749 --> 00:19:10,944
Not smart enough for you?

303
00:19:11,117 --> 00:19:13,037
I'll have you know, he
graduated from law school.

304
00:19:13,185 --> 00:19:15,620
This has nothing to
do with his law school.

305
00:19:15,788 --> 00:19:16,915
Maybe to you it don't.

306
00:19:17,223 --> 00:19:19,852
But I paid a lot of
money for that degree,

307
00:19:20,026 --> 00:19:21,585
and with your help,

308
00:19:21,761 --> 00:19:25,129
Clifford's gonna pay
me back, and then some.

309
00:19:26,899 --> 00:19:28,197
We have a deal, Ben.

310
00:19:28,367 --> 00:19:30,859
Why do you keep
saying we have a deal?

311
00:19:31,037 --> 00:19:33,871
I'm doing the best I can.
What more do you want?

312
00:19:36,042 --> 00:19:37,305
Dinner would be nice.

313
00:19:43,516 --> 00:19:46,281
I am so glad you and
Ben are doing this.

314
00:19:46,452 --> 00:19:50,048
Duncan is a dear but, uh,
can I tell you something?

315
00:19:50,222 --> 00:19:53,317
I am looking forward to not
doing someone else's laundry.

316
00:19:53,492 --> 00:19:54,824
There is that.

317
00:19:54,994 --> 00:19:56,826
I understand you're divorced?

318
00:19:57,630 --> 00:19:58,654
Separated.

319
00:19:59,598 --> 00:20:01,362
Any chance of reconciling?

320
00:20:01,534 --> 00:20:03,230
Oh, no.

321
00:20:05,705 --> 00:20:09,801
You know, to be honest, I hate
to admit that I failed at marriage.

322
00:20:10,176 --> 00:20:11,439
Exactly.

323
00:20:11,610 --> 00:20:16,014
You expect the perfect
relationship, then you're disappointed.

324
00:20:16,182 --> 00:20:18,481
I don't know if I
was expecting that.

325
00:20:18,651 --> 00:20:20,371
Of course, I wasn't
expecting him to run off

326
00:20:20,519 --> 00:20:23,580
with a 25-year-old
aerobics instructor, either.

327
00:20:23,756 --> 00:20:25,588
Oh.

328
00:20:25,758 --> 00:20:29,490
Well, if your husband acted
out his negative feelings

329
00:20:29,662 --> 00:20:31,790
instead of working
them through with you,

330
00:20:31,964 --> 00:20:33,626
perhaps you're
better off without him.

331
00:20:39,472 --> 00:20:40,531
Will you look at this?

332
00:20:41,807 --> 00:20:43,105
Lipstick.

333
00:20:47,580 --> 00:20:49,708
Glow In the dark pink.

334
00:20:49,982 --> 00:20:53,009
He lied to me. He's
still having an affair.

335
00:20:53,419 --> 00:20:55,251
Well, Clara, maybe
he got it at work.

336
00:20:55,421 --> 00:20:59,017
Maybe one of his patients
was crying on his shoulder.

337
00:20:59,191 --> 00:21:00,853
Bull.

338
00:21:01,794 --> 00:21:04,263
The SOB is having an affair.

339
00:21:06,432 --> 00:21:08,765
Duncan and I have
come up with a document

340
00:21:08,934 --> 00:21:11,802
that we think reflects
both the spirit and the letter

341
00:21:11,971 --> 00:21:13,599
of what was discussed earlier.

342
00:21:13,773 --> 00:21:15,207
That spirit has changed, Dad.

343
00:21:15,374 --> 00:21:18,105
Now all we have to
do now... How's that?

344
00:21:18,277 --> 00:21:20,178
Uh, certain behavior
has come to light,

345
00:21:20,346 --> 00:21:23,839
which substantially has changed
my client's predisposition...

346
00:21:24,016 --> 00:21:27,111
From now on, you can have
your girlfriends do your laundry.

347
00:21:28,187 --> 00:21:29,382
Now what?

348
00:21:29,555 --> 00:21:31,956
Clara thinks that Duncan
is still having an affair.

349
00:21:32,124 --> 00:21:34,923
- Oh. You are delusional.
- Don't try to gaslight me, Duncan.

350
00:21:35,094 --> 00:21:36,426
That is not my lipstick.

351
00:21:36,595 --> 00:21:39,656
- So now you're going to stick it to me.
- In spades.

352
00:21:39,832 --> 00:21:42,495
Just what is it, Clara, exactly
that you think you're gonna get?

353
00:21:42,668 --> 00:21:46,161
- What is the matter with you?
- You're in denial about our...

354
00:21:47,606 --> 00:21:49,700
We've got the papers
right here, Leanne.

355
00:21:49,875 --> 00:21:51,867
Let's just get them
to sign in a few places.

356
00:21:52,044 --> 00:21:54,036
Oh, but Clara feels that
Duncan can't be trusted.

357
00:21:54,213 --> 00:21:55,738
I don't care if she
trusts him or not.

358
00:21:55,915 --> 00:21:57,975
Let's just sign and
get them out of here.

359
00:21:58,150 --> 00:22:00,949
She thinks that if he lied about
this woman, he might be lying

360
00:22:01,120 --> 00:22:03,487
- about their joint assets too.
- You're overreacting.

361
00:22:03,656 --> 00:22:06,854
- She smells a rat, Dad.
- I don't care if she smells a skunk.

362
00:22:07,026 --> 00:22:09,066
Maybe that's why her nose
sticks up the way it does.

363
00:22:09,195 --> 00:22:11,960
They wanted a simple divorce,
and now everything's gone to hell

364
00:22:12,131 --> 00:22:14,657
because she found a
little lipstick on his shirt.

365
00:22:14,834 --> 00:22:17,030
She feels he's not the trusted
man she thought he was.

366
00:22:17,203 --> 00:22:19,798
Who cares? Let's
just get them to sign.

367
00:22:19,972 --> 00:22:22,942
Dad, I'm her lawyer and
you're Duncan's lawyer.

368
00:22:23,108 --> 00:22:25,168
- Yeah. What happened to that?
- Shut up, Duncan.

369
00:22:25,344 --> 00:22:27,108
That's very hostile of you, Ben.

370
00:22:27,279 --> 00:22:30,613
- Dad, your blood pressure.
- Damn my blood pressure.

371
00:22:30,783 --> 00:22:33,582
I never wanted anything
to do with this divorce.

372
00:22:34,053 --> 00:22:36,989
And now my blood
pressure's gone sky-high

373
00:22:37,156 --> 00:22:39,751
and my very own
daughter's turning against me.

374
00:22:39,925 --> 00:22:43,726
Dad, I am just trying to do my
job. I am not turning against you.

375
00:22:43,896 --> 00:22:47,060
Turning against me. Turning
against me. Turning against me.

376
00:22:49,201 --> 00:22:51,193
I think we'd better bring
this meeting to a close.

377
00:22:51,804 --> 00:22:53,739
But the papers.

378
00:22:53,906 --> 00:22:56,899
Ah, we have to
re-evaluate things.

379
00:23:01,380 --> 00:23:03,349
I'll see you later.

380
00:23:03,649 --> 00:23:05,880
Well, now you're mad.

381
00:23:07,319 --> 00:23:09,550
- Oh, I'm not mad.
- Yes, you are. You're mad.

382
00:23:09,722 --> 00:23:12,419
All right, I'm mad.
I'll see you later.

383
00:23:12,591 --> 00:23:15,390
Well, aren't you gonna stay
and have something to eat?

384
00:23:15,561 --> 00:23:17,655
I'll fix something good.

385
00:23:17,830 --> 00:23:22,165
There's some eggs and grits
and maybe some sardines.

386
00:23:29,141 --> 00:23:32,407
What's that smell?
What's burning?

387
00:23:37,850 --> 00:23:40,376
Oh, my God. That's my book.

388
00:23:40,552 --> 00:23:42,748
Ha, ha. Burns like
a bandit, doesn't it?

389
00:23:43,289 --> 00:23:45,315
Get back.

390
00:23:45,958 --> 00:23:48,359
It's my side and I am
prepared to defend it.

391
00:23:55,567 --> 00:23:58,935
Château Pauillac 1976.

392
00:23:59,104 --> 00:24:02,700
Château La Serre
1958. Cute names.

393
00:24:02,875 --> 00:24:05,902
Cute? Those wines are priceless.

394
00:24:06,078 --> 00:24:07,671
Good. Unh!

395
00:24:09,982 --> 00:24:11,974
- Clara.
- Ah.

396
00:24:12,151 --> 00:24:15,986
That design would make
a lovely wallpaper. Unh!

397
00:24:22,428 --> 00:24:25,057
We're going to have to
get going here, Mr. Matlock.

398
00:24:25,230 --> 00:24:26,698
Uh, yes, sir.

399
00:24:26,865 --> 00:24:29,130
Uh, she should be here
any minute, Your Honor.

400
00:24:29,301 --> 00:24:31,395
I'm sorry. It's just...

401
00:24:33,772 --> 00:24:35,900
I'm dead.

402
00:24:40,879 --> 00:24:43,110
I'm sorry to bother you, Ben.

403
00:24:44,683 --> 00:24:46,709
What are you doing here?

404
00:24:46,885 --> 00:24:48,820
I'm here to see Leanne.

405
00:24:49,688 --> 00:24:52,385
- Why?
- Uh, there she is.

406
00:24:52,558 --> 00:24:54,584
Nice of you to join
us, Ms. Mclntyre.

407
00:24:54,760 --> 00:24:58,162
I hope this isn't a preview of
how you plan to conduct this case.

408
00:24:58,330 --> 00:25:00,196
My sincere
apologies, Your Honor.

409
00:25:00,366 --> 00:25:02,562
If you could just grant
me a minute's more grace?

410
00:25:02,735 --> 00:25:05,170
One minute, no more.

411
00:25:07,773 --> 00:25:09,105
What are you doing?

412
00:25:09,274 --> 00:25:11,766
We're not even up to bat and
there are two strikes against us.

413
00:25:11,944 --> 00:25:15,278
My car wouldn't start. Dad, I
need to speak to Cliff alone.

414
00:25:15,981 --> 00:25:19,315
- Alone?
- Please, Dad?

415
00:25:19,485 --> 00:25:22,080
- What...? What...?
- Dad, please?

416
00:25:25,524 --> 00:25:28,187
- So you said you had a job for me.
- Yeah.

417
00:25:28,360 --> 00:25:31,330
Um, all the details are in here.
Don't open it until you get outside.

418
00:25:31,930 --> 00:25:33,728
And don't tell my
father about it either.

419
00:25:33,899 --> 00:25:35,765
You're working for
me on this one, okay?

420
00:25:35,934 --> 00:25:37,774
Are you sure this
is all right with Ben?

421
00:25:37,836 --> 00:25:39,429
Time's up, Ms. Mclntyre.

422
00:25:39,605 --> 00:25:41,301
Ready, Your Honor.

423
00:25:41,874 --> 00:25:42,898
I'm sure.

424
00:25:49,348 --> 00:25:51,579
You should have settled
while you had a chance.

425
00:25:52,251 --> 00:25:54,948
Court is now in session.

426
00:25:55,854 --> 00:25:58,585
You're recently married,
aren't you, Mr. Fuqua?

427
00:25:58,757 --> 00:26:01,852
Yes, sir. About six months ago.

428
00:26:02,628 --> 00:26:07,896
And how would you characterize
your relationship with your wife,

429
00:26:08,067 --> 00:26:10,298
uh, since the accident?

430
00:26:11,770 --> 00:26:13,238
Uh, not so good.

431
00:26:13,405 --> 00:26:15,340
Could you be a
little more specific?

432
00:26:20,245 --> 00:26:23,477
I can't make love to
Suzanne like I used to.

433
00:26:23,649 --> 00:26:25,174
What else?

434
00:26:25,350 --> 00:26:28,047
She's gotta do a
lot of stuff for me

435
00:26:28,220 --> 00:26:31,247
that, uh, a man
wants to do for himself.

436
00:26:32,458 --> 00:26:36,691
At this point, I'd
like to show the court

437
00:26:36,862 --> 00:26:40,822
a videotape shot by an
independent filmmaker

438
00:26:40,999 --> 00:26:43,798
who is prepared to
testify here to its veracity.

439
00:26:44,269 --> 00:26:47,865
This is a typical day
in the life of Carl Fuqua.

440
00:26:49,408 --> 00:26:52,139
Will you tell us about
your life, Mr. Fuqua?

441
00:26:53,178 --> 00:26:54,908
Sure.

442
00:26:56,548 --> 00:26:59,245
Uh, here I am in bed.

443
00:26:59,418 --> 00:27:01,284
I used to be a real early riser.

444
00:27:01,453 --> 00:27:03,213
But now with all the
pain pills I gotta take,

445
00:27:03,355 --> 00:27:05,017
Suzanne has to take care of me.

446
00:27:07,593 --> 00:27:11,894
It's like I'm her kid,
not her husband.

447
00:27:12,064 --> 00:27:16,559
She's even gotta help
me go to the bathroom.

448
00:27:16,735 --> 00:27:18,966
Objection, Your Honor.
How can we be sure

449
00:27:19,138 --> 00:27:22,336
that this whole tape wasn't
staged for the benefit of camera?

450
00:27:22,508 --> 00:27:27,310
Future witnesses will testify as to
the serious nature of my client's injury.

451
00:27:27,479 --> 00:27:30,745
His wife and a
filmmaker will corroborate

452
00:27:30,916 --> 00:27:34,876
as to this being a typical
day in the life of Carl Fuqua.

453
00:27:35,053 --> 00:27:40,492
This is nothing but a shameful
display to influence the jury.

454
00:27:40,659 --> 00:27:43,094
- And you know it.
- Mr. Matlock.

455
00:27:43,262 --> 00:27:47,495
But, Your Honor, this whole
tape, this is just made-up hogwash.

456
00:27:47,666 --> 00:27:50,534
You have an attorney.
Please, speak through her.

457
00:27:50,702 --> 00:27:51,726
But, Your Honor...

458
00:27:52,504 --> 00:27:54,473
Speak through her.

459
00:27:56,208 --> 00:27:57,506
Objection overruled.

460
00:27:59,678 --> 00:28:02,307
You may proceed, Mr. Fuqua.

461
00:28:04,616 --> 00:28:06,084
Uh...

462
00:28:06,251 --> 00:28:10,347
Every morning, Suzanne
helps me get dressed.

463
00:28:11,056 --> 00:28:13,525
Not that I go anywhere,

464
00:28:13,692 --> 00:28:17,925
but, well, a man does what he
can to keep his dignity, you know.

465
00:28:18,730 --> 00:28:21,199
I mean, she's my wife
and I know she loves me,

466
00:28:21,366 --> 00:28:24,768
but how do you ask
somebody to sacrifice like that?

467
00:28:24,937 --> 00:28:28,931
I look into her eyes and I see
the pain and it hurts so much.

468
00:28:29,107 --> 00:28:30,837
Would you like a moment - Speak.

469
00:28:31,009 --> 00:28:32,987
- To compose yourself?
- Say something. Object.

470
00:28:33,011 --> 00:28:34,171
I did. JUDGE: Ms. Mclntyre.

471
00:28:34,313 --> 00:28:35,337
Object again.

472
00:28:35,514 --> 00:28:37,881
Dad, believe it or not,
I know what I'm doing.

473
00:28:38,050 --> 00:28:40,019
You're letting this
tearjerker bury me.

474
00:28:41,553 --> 00:28:42,953
Mr. Matlock.

475
00:28:43,121 --> 00:28:46,182
My sincere apologies, Your
Honor. It won't happen again.

476
00:28:47,025 --> 00:28:49,722
I know a daughter is not supposed
to speak to her father this way,

477
00:28:49,895 --> 00:28:51,727
but you leave me no choice.

478
00:28:51,897 --> 00:28:53,195
Shut up.

479
00:29:07,813 --> 00:29:09,748
What have we done?

480
00:29:10,115 --> 00:29:17,682
We have acted out all the
hostility we repressed for years.

481
00:29:18,957 --> 00:29:21,358
- I feel so free.
- Ha, ha.

482
00:29:22,527 --> 00:29:26,122
You know, come to think of it,

483
00:29:26,298 --> 00:29:29,325
I feel like a great weight has
been lifted off my shoulders.

484
00:29:31,136 --> 00:29:36,268
I think we are going to be
better people, better therapists.

485
00:29:36,441 --> 00:29:39,001
- I may take a younger lover.
- Ha, ha.

486
00:29:40,178 --> 00:29:41,976
- Ah.
- Ooh.

487
